WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.480
Cross a red snapdragon with a white one.

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.520
In the classic textbook example, every offspring has pink flowers.

3
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.440
Breed a red Shorthorn cow with a white bull.

4
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:21.040
The calf is roan. Its coat has red hairs and white hairs side by side.

5
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.880
These do not fit the old rule, where a dominant allele hides a recessive one.

6
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.500
What is going on?

7
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:32.053
An allele is one version of a gene.

8
00:00:32.793 --> 00:00:35.693
A heterozygous plant has two different alleles.

9
00:00:36.353 --> 00:00:41.413
For snapdragon color, R is the red allele, and W is the white allele.

10
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:47.493
RR is red, WW is white, and RW is pink.

11
00:00:48.313 --> 00:00:50.033
This is incomplete dominance.

12
00:00:50.713 --> 00:00:53.093
The heterozygote shows a color in between.

13
00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:55.807
Think of mixing paint.

14
00:00:56.307 --> 00:00:58.127
But paint stays mixed.

15
00:00:58.987 --> 00:01:03.827
A pink plant passes a separate R or W to each sex cell.

16
00:01:04.527 --> 00:01:10.267
Cross two pink plants, and the Punnett square gives one red, two pink, and one

17
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:12.947
white. Red and white come back.

18
00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:15.047
The alleles never blended.

19
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.790
Codominance means both alleles show fully in the heterozygote.

20
00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.230
In Shorthorn cattle, RW is roan.

21
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:30.410
Each hair is red or white, and the two kinds grow side by side.

22
00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:37.130
A roan by roan cross gives one red, two roan, one white, on average.

23
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.670
Human ABO blood type is another case.

24
00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:46.510
Red blood cells can carry an A marker, a B marker, both, or neither.

25
00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.110
Type AB carries both.

26
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:52.893
Geneticists study existing families.

27
00:01:53.673 --> 00:01:55.613
A pedigree is a chart of a family.

28
00:01:56.473 --> 00:01:59.033
A shaded symbol is a person who shows the trait.

29
00:01:59.913 --> 00:02:05.233
Two unshaded parents with a shaded child are a clue for a recessive trait, not

30
00:02:05.233 --> 00:02:10.793
proof. A karyotype is a picture of a cell's chromosomes, sorted into pairs.

31
00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:14.473
Most human body cells have 46 chromosomes.

32
00:02:14.473 --> 00:02:22.373
Three copies of chromosome 21 is called trisomy 21, and a cell with it has 47 chromosomes.

33
00:02:22.783 --> 00:02:26.003
So what if neither allele is fully dominant?

34
00:02:26.843 --> 00:02:30.623
In incomplete dominance, the heterozygote is in between.

35
00:02:31.543 --> 00:02:34.243
In codominance, it shows both alleles.

36
00:02:35.043 --> 00:02:39.583
Either way, the alleles stay separate, and red and white offspring return.

37
00:02:40.863 --> 00:02:45.023
Pedigrees and karyotypes let us follow traits through families and chromosomes.

